Declare this system to WTF: OS(LIKEOS).

Until now the engine compiled here only because it found `unix' defined and
took the generic POSIX arms.  That was enough for a cairo-rendering,
interpreter-only build, and it is not enough for this one: the JIT and the
sampling profiler need to read and write the machine context out of a signal
frame, and the code that does so is selected by operating system, not by a
feature test.  There is no arm this system can take, so HAVE_MACHINE_CONTEXT
stays undefined and the profiler is compiled out -- silently.

So the platform gets a name.  OS(LIKEOS) is defined from __LIKEOS__ (which
the port's compiler wrapper defines) and joins OS(UNIX), which it already
qualified for through `unix'.  Nothing that was taking a POSIX arm changes.

The five capabilities it is then added to are each true here, and each was
verified rather than assumed:

  HAVE_MACHINE_CONTEXT -- the kernel now delivers a real ucontext_t as the
    third argument of a SA_SIGINFO handler, with the conventional x86-64
    gregs[] layout and REG_* indices, and sigreturn restores what the handler
    edited (kernel/ke/signal.c).  That is exactly what the OS(LINUX) arm of
    MachineContext.h reads, so that arm is extended rather than copied.
  HAVE_MADV_FREE / HAVE_MADV_DONTNEED -- both are implemented
    (kernel/mm/madvise.c).
  HAVE_DLADDR -- the dynamic loader answers it (user/lib/rtld).
  HAVE_FALLOCATE -- implemented (kernel/fs/read_write.c).
  HAVE_INT128_T -- a compiler property on a 64-bit target, listed by OS
    upstream; the compiler is the same GCC.

InlineASM.h gets the same treatment for a different reason: the LLInt is
generated as inline assembly, and the local-label syntax and the directive
that hides a symbol are chosen there by operating system too.  With no arm
matching, LOCAL_LABEL_STRING went undefined and the generated
LLIntAssembly.h did not compile at all ("expected â)â before
âLOCAL_LABEL_STRINGâ").  The object format here is ELF and the
assembler is GNU as, so this system belongs in the arm that says so.

